        "math_academy_id": "q-351194",
        "problem": "Express $\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}$ as a sum of partial fractions.\n\n| $\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}=$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | {{field-2}} |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $x+1$ |  | $(x+1)^{2}$ |",
        "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{A}{x+1}+\\frac{B}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the above equality by $(x+1)^{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n5x-6=A(x+1)+B.\n$$\n\nLetting $x=-1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-1)-6 & =A(-1+1)+B \\\\ B & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $x=0$ and using our value for $B$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-6 & =A(0+1)-11 \\\\ -6 & =A-11 \\\\ A & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x-6}{(x+1)^{2}}=\\frac{5}{x+1}-\\frac{11}{(x+1)^{2}}.\n$$",
